  "query_preview": "..High pressure remains centered over the OK/TX\nPanhandles and northeast NM this evening. Utah saw a slight \ninflux of moisture today, with PWATs across southern Utah climbing\nto 1-1.2 inches while northern Utah remained under 1 inch. Aided \nby this increased moisture and a subtle vort lobe rotating around \nthe high and lifting through Utah, increased shower and \nthunderstorm activity was noted today. While thunderstorms are \nwaning with the loss of daytime heating, rain showers, generally \nlight, will continue to spread into northern Utah tonight as the \nvort lobe continues to lift north, while they taper off across \nsouthern Utah. These showers will continue through the day on \nMonday across northern Utah, along with isolated thunderstorms in\nthe afternoon. Southern Utah should see generally isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms on Monday.\n\nBy Tuesday, the high pressure will shift closer to the Four\nCorners region, bringing a more southerly flow to southern Utah \nand Arizona, which should help spread more moisture into the area\nand allowing for increased convective coverage. However, this \nrenewed moisture surge will be brief, as the high will continue to\ngradually shift westward through the week. Towards the end of the\nweek, the high pressure will be centered too far west to favor the\nmonsoon pattern, leading to drier conditions. This drying trend\nshould begin on Thursday or Friday and will likely extend through\nat least the first half of next week. Cluster analysis of global\nensembles are in good agreement on the general large-scale\nevolution. \n\nOtherwise, hot temperatures will remain in place areawide through\nthe week, with most valley areas seeing highs hovering near or \nabove 100F. Conditions will be exacerbated tonight through \nMonday with cloud cover helping to maintain warm overnight lows. \nIncreasing precipitation midweek is expected to cool the airmass \nenough to reduce HeatRisk to mostly Moderate in the valleys, but \nwhen the airmass dries out again late week with strong high \npressure overhead, HeatRisk will increase again, with widespread \nareas of Major to Extreme HeatRisk noted.",
